      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability that the sum of the numbers shown on two standard 6-sided dice is a prime number. We start by identifying all possible outcomes when two dice are tossed. Each die has 6 faces, so there are \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\) possible outcomes.\n\nNext, we list all the possible sums of the numbers on the two dice and determine which of these sums are prime numbers. The possible sums range from \\(2\\) to \\(12\\). The prime numbers in this range are \\(2, 3, 5, 7,\\) and \\(11\\).\n\nWe will count the number of ways to achieve each of these prime sums:\n\n1. **Sum = 2**: The only combination is \\((1, 1)\\). There is 1 way.\n2. **Sum = 3**: The combinations are \\((1, 2)\\) and \\((2, 1)\\). There are 2 ways.\n3. **Sum = 5**: The combinations are \\((1, 4)\\), \\((2, 3)\\), \\((3, 2)\\), and \\((4, 1)\\). There are 4 ways.\n4. **Sum = 7**: The combinations are \\((1, 6)\\), \\((2, 5)\\), \\((3, 4)\\), \\((4, 3)\\), \\((5, 2)\\), and \\((6, 1)\\). There are 6 ways.\n5. **Sum = 11**: The combinations are \\((5, 6)\\) and \\((6, 5)\\). There are 2 ways.\n\nAdding these, we get the total number of favorable outcomes:\n\\[1 + 2 + 4 + 6 + 2 = 15.\\]\n\nThe probability that the sum of the numbers shown on the dice is a prime number is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{36} = \\frac{5}{12}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that the sum of the numbers shown on the dice is a prime number is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{12}}\\).",
